The Plaza
[vignette dungeons/the_city]

FIXME(mundane): PC enters the central plaza of an alien domed city (Revathi or Revathi-ancestral). Torben is sketching in his notebook, muttering. The plaza is the hub of a radial corridor plan: five major arteries plus service passages, all leading deeper. Dome-light comes from above — sourceless, directionless, not torch-fire, not sun. The walls and floors are a single seamless material throughout, no seams or joins visible.

FIXME(mundane): At the plaza entrance stands a geometric statue on a plinth — angular, non-representational, no human or animal forms. Torben identifies it as Revathi devotional geometry: planes and angles, placed to observe whoever enters. The statue reads as a sentinel or overseer, not decoration.

FIXME(mundane): The commons (down one corridor): a long room with two rows of tables fixed to the floor on metal stems, benches either side. Dust undisturbed across every surface. At the far end, a counter and a preparation area with flat work surfaces, recessed basins with drainage channels, dark wall-panels (heating elements or unknown). Torben carries a lantern; the dome-light does not reach here. Everything is where you would expect a kitchen to put it. Nothing is a size or shape you would expect.

FIXME(mundane): The exchange (another corridor): a high-ceilinged hall split by a long counter. One side open floor (waiting/queue area). Other side: work-stations built into the wall, each with recessed shelf and writing surface. Behind them, heavy industrial shelving and a loading bay with a sealed wide door.

FIXME(dread): The watchtower stair: each step shallow and wide, built for a stride longer than a human's. Torben's lantern barely reaches the curved walls. Air dries and thins as you climb; a faint ventilation current from somewhere above. From the top platform the city is far larger than it appeared — radial streets extend to the dome wall in every direction. The dome glass curves close overhead.

FIXME(dread): The dome-light is sourceless. Torben notes it explicitly ("assuming the light fades — assuming it isn't something else") and cannot account for it. PC is from a renaissance-era world; steady, even, cool light from an unseen source is well outside their frame of reference. Torben is present throughout as the theorizing companion (notebook, pencil, lantern, cramped annotations).
